From a976988d0e41f20950baf6df5c2f0c5ead1df8af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bruce Ashfield <bruce.ashfield@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 13:46:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/10] sandbox: add vxn RuntimeProvider (Xen DomU isolation
 backend)

AXIS's Linux isolation is process-level (bubblewrap/Landlock/seccomp on the host
kernel). For high-risk agents a stronger boundary is a whole VM: run the
sandboxed command inside a Xen DomU, where a guest-kernel exploit cannot reach
the host and denied resources simply do not exist in the guest.

Add RuntimeProvider::Vxn, selected on Linux as the LinuxVxn backend, implemented
by a new VxnSandbox that drives the vxn CLI. A foreground `vxn run` maps directly
onto the Child-shaped SandboxImpl trait: start->pid, wait->exit code,
take_std*->the child's stdio, destroy->kill. VXN_BIN selects the vxn binary (host
CLI fronting a nested dom0, or the in-dom0 vxn), VXN_BASE_IMAGE the DomU rootfs.

Network policy maps to the VM boundary: Block -> --no-network (no vif at all),
Allow -> bridge. Proxy (the default) fails closed rather than silently
downgrading to allow -- vxn has no AXIS-proxy path yet, and the isolation
contract forbids reporting weaker behaviour as exact enforcement. On non-Linux
the provider is rejected.

The AXIS process primitives are kernel-local and do not cross into the DomU's
separate kernel, so they are not applied here; the VM boundary enforces the
coarse policy structurally. Filesystem mounts, env transport, the rw workspace,
and nested in-guest enforcement are follow-ups.

Signed-off-by: Bruce Ashfield <bruce.ashfield@gmail.com>
